    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can you dive a bit more into that \"nervous system overload\" part? i'm trying to explain it to my client so it doesn't just sound like i'm making excuses for why they aren't getting stronger. how does that actually manifest in their training sessions?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Nervous system overload means your body can't efficiently recruit maximum muscle fibers, even if the muscle tissue itself isn't fully fatigued. In training, this manifests as a noticeable drop in power output or perceived effort, where the movement feels \"heavy\" even with the same weight. It often presents as a decrease in the quality of the lift, reduced bar speed, or a sudden need to significantly cut down on overall volume.",
      "source": "target",
